const e='<p>Set the display up first with <a href="/guides/connect-iphone-ipad-to-monitor-or-tv">the connection guide</a>. In a meeting room you\'ll usually want USB-C to HDMI, or a Lightning Digital AV Adapter on older iPhones. Many rooms still have VGA only, a Lightning to VGA adapter covers that.</p>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p><strong>Before you travel:</strong> export your deck to PDF and put it somewhere you can reach offline, the app\'s own Files, or a folder you\'ve mounted. Conference Wi-Fi fails; a PDF on the device doesn\'t. Method 2 below is the one that works with no network at all.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>Method 1, Google Slides (or PowerPoint on the web)</h2>\n\n  <p>The browser in XeOS is a desktop browser, so slides.google.com behaves the way it does on a computer, including presenter controls.</p>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>Open the <strong>Browser</strong> and go to <code>slides.google.com</code> (or <code>office.com</code> for PowerPoint).</li>\n    <li>Sign in. If you <a href="/guides/import-bookmarks-and-passwords">imported your passwords</a> this takes seconds.</li>\n    <li>Open your deck and start the slideshow from Slides\' own Present control.</li>\n    <li>Click the <strong>green button</strong> in the window\'s corner so the window itself goes full screen. Now there is no title bar, no tab strip, no dock, just your slides on the projector.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <figure class="is-inline">\n    <img src="/content/images/traffic-lights.webp" alt="The window buttons, with the green full-screen button third from the left." width="352" height="118" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>The green button removes every piece of interface from the screen.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Advance slides with a Bluetooth keyboard\'s arrow keys, with a mouse click, or by tapping your phone\'s screen as a trackpad. Move the pointer to the top edge to bring the title bar back when you\'re finished.</p>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p>If a control inside Google\'s interface won\'t respond to a click, it\'s the embedded-frame limitation covered in <a href="/guides/device-layout-mode">Device Layout Mode</a>: tap the grey button, do that one action on the phone screen, and switch straight back to the projector.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>Method 2, The built-in viewer (a PDF, no network needed)</h2>\n\n  <p>This is the reliable one. Export your deck to PDF before you leave, and you can present from the device with no Wi-Fi, no login and nothing to load.</p>\n\n  <h3>Get the file onto the device</h3>\n\n  <p>Any of these works:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Import from your phone</strong>, the Files app has an Import button that opens the iOS document picker.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mount a folder</strong>, point XeOS at an iCloud Drive folder, a folder on your phone, or a USB drive, and open the file in place. See <a href="/guides/mount-icloud-local-and-external-drives">the drives guide</a>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Download it</strong>, from email or the web, straight into Downloads.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Open it and go full screen</h3>\n\n  <p>Double-click the PDF. It opens in the built-in viewer, which handles page navigation, zoom, a thumbnail sidebar, search, and annotation.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/pdf-viewer.webp" alt="The XeOS PDF viewer showing a presentation slide, with a toolbar across the top." width="1600" height="1006" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>The PDF viewer, windowed. Sidebar, zoom, search and annotation tools across the top.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Then click the green button. The slide fills the projector completely:</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <span class="device-frame"><svg class="device-frame-chrome" viewBox="0 0 320 200" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="0" y="0" width="320" height="184" rx="3" class="device-frame-bezel"/><path d="M146 184 L141 197 L179 197 L174 184 Z" class="device-frame-neck"/><rect x="118" y="196" width="84" height="4" rx="2" class="device-frame-base"/></svg><span class="device-frame-screen"><img src="/content/images/pdf-fullscreen.webp" alt="A presentation slide filling an entire external display." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></span></span>\n    <figcaption>Full screen, with the whole slide edge to edge.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Page through with the arrow keys, Page Up / Page Down, a scroll wheel, or a swipe on the phone-as-trackpad.</p>\n\n  <h3>Getting the slide to fill the frame</h3>\n\n  <p>Slide decks exported to PDF are usually 16:9, which matches most projectors and screens exactly. If your deck is 4:3, you\'ll see a band on each side, that\'s the deck\'s shape, not the app\'s. Set the viewer\'s zoom to fit the page width, and if the projector is adding its own border on top of that, check its picture-size setting (look for "Just Scan", "Screen Fit" or "1:1").</p>\n\n  <h2>Presenting other file types</h2>\n\n  <p>XeOS also previews Word, Excel and PowerPoint files directly, useful when someone hands you a <code>.pptx</code> on a USB stick five minutes beforehand. It\'s a preview: fidelity is good enough to present from, but for anything where exact layout matters, PDF is safer.</p>\n\n  <h2>A short pre-flight checklist</h2>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Deck exported to PDF and on the device.</li>\n    <li>Adapter <em>and</em> a spare HDMI cable in the bag, rooms often have the cable bolted down and the wrong end free.</li>\n    <li>Multiport adapter if the talk is long, so the phone charges while it presents.</li>\n    <li>Do Not Disturb on, so a message doesn\'t appear on a four-metre screen.</li>\n    <li>Auto-lock off, in iOS Settings › Display &amp; Brightness.</li>\n    <li>Content scale checked in Settings › Display, a room reads better slightly larger.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, TV or projector.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Common questions</h2>\n\n    <h3>Can I see presenter notes on my phone while slides show on the projector?</h3>\n    <p>Not as a separate presenter view, the external display and the phone show the same session, and the phone acts as your trackpad. The practical approach is notes on paper, or a second device.</p>\n\n    <h3>Does it work with old projectors?</h3>\n    <p>Yes, over VGA with a Lightning to VGA adapter, or HDMI on anything newer. Older projectors often prefer 1280 × 720 or 1920 × 1080, you can pick the mode in Settings › Display.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I annotate a PDF while presenting?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. The viewer has annotation tools, and changes can be saved back to the file in place.</p>\n\n    <h3>What if the room only has Wi-Fi and no cable?</h3>\n    <p>If there\'s an Apple TV or AirPlay-capable screen, mirror to it from Control Centre and XeOS will pick up the display automatically.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
